Chapter 13
Pricing over the product life cycle
التسعير عبر دورة حياة المنتج
A price is not set once. The price that launches a product is rarely the price that defends it five years later, and the reason is that the market around it changes: competitors arrive, customers learn, costs fall with experience, and the product that was novel becomes ordinary.
This chapter sets the pricing decisions of Chapters 10 to 12 into time. It distinguishes the short-run decision, where capacity and costs are given, from the long-run one, where they are not; describes the four stages a product passes through; sets out the pricing strategies that suit each; and ends with the recommendation the outcome asks for.
Figure F713-01 is your map.
Figure F713-01. Chapter 13 roadmap: two horizons, four stages, and the strategies that fit each.
