WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 6 computed a relevant-cost floor of $7.60 for a one-off contract and said it was not a basis for regular pricing. Chapter 10 computed a full-cost floor of $10.81 and said it was a long-run minimum. This chapter explains why the two floors belong to different horizons, and what happens between them.
13.1  Two horizons, two floors
A short-term pricing decision takes the capacity, the fixed costs and the product as given, and asks only whether a particular piece of business is worth taking. Its floor is the relevant cost of Chapter 6: the incremental cost plus any contribution displaced. Below that floor the company is worse off; above it, better off, by the contribution the business brings.
A long-term pricing decision asks what a product must earn to justify its existence. Over a long enough period every cost is avoidable, as Chapter 1 established: the plant can be sold, the lease ended, the staff redeployed. So the long-run floor is the full cost, including the fixed costs, and including a return on the capital employed — which is the target-return price of Chapter 10.
Four consequences follow, and each is examinable.
Short-run prices below full cost are legitimate and sometimes necessary: a special order with idle capacity, a launch price intended to win position, a clearance price for goods that will otherwise be written off. The test is whether the business covers its own incremental cost and whether it damages the regular market.
A company cannot price below full cost indefinitely. Every product priced on a short-run basis is being subsidised by the rest of the range, and a company whose whole range is priced that way is making a loss.
The horizon the question specifies determines the cost to use. "A one-off order" means relevant cost; "our standard price list" means full cost and a return.
And the two converge over time, because in the long run the fixed costs of the short run become decisions again.
Figure F713-02 sets the two out on Orontes's own figures.
Figure F713-02. Two horizons, two floors
@table
| Short-term decision | Long-term decision
Capacity and fixed costs are | Given | Decisions
The floor is | Incremental cost plus displaced contribution | Full cost plus a return on capital
For Orontes's jar | 7.20, or 12.00 at full capacity | 12.06 at a 15 per cent target return
Suits | Special orders, clearance, launch pricing | The price list, contract renewals, new products
Danger | Repeating it until it is the business | Pricing above what the market will pay
@end
