13.2  The four stages
A product life cycle describes the sales of a product from launch to withdrawal, and the exam names four stages.
Introduction. Sales are low and growing slowly, because customers do not know the product and distribution is incomplete. Costs per unit are high, since volume is small and the learning has not happened. Competition is light or absent. Cash flow is negative: development has been paid for and the launch costs money.
Growth. Sales accelerate as the product becomes known and distribution widens. Unit costs fall with volume and experience. Competitors arrive, attracted by the growth, usually with improved versions. Profits are at their highest, and so is the need for working capital to fund the growth — which is the sustainable growth problem of Book 1, Chapter 15, arriving at product level.
Maturity. Sales growth slows and then stops as the market saturates. Competition is at its fiercest, because growth can now only be taken from somebody else, so prices fall and differentiation shifts to brand, service and small improvements. Costs per unit are at their lowest. Cash flow is strongest, because the growth no longer consumes working capital. Most products spend most of their lives here.
Decline. Sales fall, as tastes change or a better technology arrives. Competitors leave. Prices may fall further, or in some cases rise, as the remaining buyers become a captive niche. The decision becomes whether to harvest the product — minimum investment, maximum cash — or to withdraw it, which is the drop-a-segment analysis of Chapter 9.
Figure F713-03 sets the stages out.
Figure F713-03. Four stages, and what changes in each
@table
Stage | Sales | Unit cost | Competition | Cash flow | Typical price
Introduction | Low, growing slowly | High | Little | Negative | High, or deliberately low
Growth | Accelerating | Falling | Increasing | Negative to positive | Falling
Maturity | Flat | Lowest | Intense | Strongest | Lowest, competitive
Decline | Falling | Rising as volume falls | Falling as rivals leave | Positive, declining | Falling, or raised for a niche
@end
Two warnings belong with the model.
The stages are a description, not a prediction. A product's sales may fall and recover, a mature product can be relaunched into a new growth phase, and the length of each stage varies from months to decades.
The stage is not always obvious at the time. Flat sales may be maturity or a bad year, and the cost of mistaking the second for the first is a product harvested that should have been invested in.
