13.4  The other strategies
Beyond the two launch strategies, a short list of pricing tactics appears in questions and each has a condition attached.
Bundling sells several products for one price below the sum of the separate prices. It works where customers value the components differently — one buyer wants the sauce, another the bottle opener — so a bundle captures more from each than separate prices would. It also hides the price of the individual items, which makes comparison harder.
Price discrimination charges different customers different prices for the same product: by segment, by time, by channel or by quantity. It is profitable wherever the groups have different elasticities and can be kept apart, and it is restricted by law in many jurisdictions where it harms competition. A student discount, an off-peak fare and a quantity rebate are all examples.
Peak-load pricing charges more when capacity is scarce — electricity in the evening, hotels in the season, delivery at the weekend. It is the clearest practical application of the opportunity cost of Chapter 6: the price should carry the contribution displaced, and when nothing is displaced the price can fall to the incremental cost.
Promotional pricing cuts the price temporarily to attract trial, clear stock, or answer a rival. Its danger is that temporary becomes expected, and Book 1's finding about Orontes's gross margin is what a long series of temporary cuts looks like in the accounts.
A loss leader is priced below cost to draw customers who buy other things. It works only where the other purchases are real and attributable, which makes it a question about the whole basket rather than the product.
Predatory pricing is pricing below cost to drive a competitor out and raise prices afterwards. It is unlawful in most jurisdictions, and the practical test regulators use is whether the price is below the seller's own avoidable cost — which makes the relevant-cost analysis of Chapter 6 a compliance question as well as a management one.
Figure F713-05 sets the conditions out.
Figure F713-05. Six tactics and the condition each needs
@table
Tactic | Works when | Watch for
Bundling | Customers value the components differently | Hiding a weak product inside a bundle
Price discrimination | Groups have different elasticities and stay separate | Legal restrictions; arbitrage between the groups
Peak-load pricing | Capacity is scarce at some times and not others | Customer resentment; measuring the peak correctly
Promotional pricing | Trial or clearance is genuinely needed | Temporary becoming permanent
Loss leader | The other purchases are real and attributable | Customers who buy only the leader
Predatory pricing | Never; it is unlawful | Prices below avoidable cost, whatever the intention
@end
