Chapter summary
Figure F713-07 brings the chapter together.
Figure F713-07. Chapter 13 summary map: the key rule for each section.
Two horizons give two floors: a short-term decision takes capacity as given and may price down to the incremental cost plus any contribution displaced, while a long-term one must cover full cost and a return on capital — and a company pricing its whole range on the short-run floor is making a loss. A product passes through introduction, growth, maturity and decline, with unit costs highest at the start and lowest at maturity, cash flow most negative at launch and strongest at maturity, and competition fiercest where growth has stopped. A launch takes one of two strategies: skimming starts high and works down, suiting inelastic early demand and a genuine lead; penetration starts low to win scale, suiting elastic demand and falling costs — and on the premium jar's estimates penetration earns 1,557,000 + 1,935,000 = 3,492,000 over two years against 450,000 + 1,440,000 = 1,890,000 for skimming, a difference of 1,602,000 that rests entirely on volume assumptions. Bundling, price discrimination, peak-load pricing, promotion and loss leaders each have a condition, and predatory pricing has none, being tested by whether the price is below the seller's own avoidable cost. And a recommendation names both floors, what the market bears, the elasticity, the life-cycle stage, the effect on everything else, and the volume at which it would reverse — which for the premium jar is 571,429 jars.
