Case-style practice: a mature product under attack
Orontes's standard tahini jar is a mature product: sales of 3,500,000 jars at $12.00, a variable cost of $7.20, fixed costs of $12,624,000 and a gross margin that Book 1 found falling a point a year. A rival launches at $10.90 with heavy promotion, and Orontes's sales director asks to match the price. Elasticity in this range was estimated in Chapter 11 at about 1.93.
Required. Evaluate matching the price, with figures. Recommend, naming two alternatives to a price cut and the figure at which your recommendation would change. Under 300 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC13-1  Answer A. The short-run floor is the relevant cost.
B is wrong. Full cost is the long-run floor.
C is wrong. That is the long-run target price.
D is wrong. The market price is a ceiling, not a floor.
SC13-2  Answer A. Nothing covers the fixed costs.
B is wrong. The company would be losing money.
C is wrong. It is not unlawful unless predatory.
D is wrong. The long run requires full cost.
SC13-3  Answer A. In the long run every cost is a decision.
B is wrong. Variable costs do not rise by virtue of the horizon.
C is wrong. The point is that fixed costs become avoidable.
D is wrong. Capacity is a decision, not a given.
SC13-4  Answer A. Volume is highest and learning complete.
B is wrong. Early volumes are small and costs high.
C is wrong. Costs are still falling in growth.
D is wrong. Falling volume raises unit cost again.
SC13-5  Answer A. Development and launch are paid before sales arrive.
B is wrong. Maturity is the strongest cash stage.
C is wrong. Decline still produces cash.
D is wrong. Harvesting is a decline-stage strategy that produces cash.
SC13-6  Answer A. With the market flat, share comes from rivals.
B is wrong. Costs are lowest at maturity.
C is wrong. Novelty belongs to introduction.
D is wrong. Rivals leave in decline.
SC13-7  Answer A. Inelastic early demand supports a high price.
B is wrong. Elastic demand suits penetration.
C is wrong. Unknown demand argues for testing, not for skimming.
D is wrong. Falling demand is a decline-stage condition.
SC13-8  Answer A. Elastic demand and scale economies reward volume.
B is wrong. Inelastic demand suits skimming.
C is wrong. The absence of competitors supports a high price.
D is wrong. Limited capacity makes volume strategies impossible.
SC13-9  Answer A. Expectations are hard to reverse.
B is wrong. Deterring entry is an advantage of the strategy.
C is wrong. Volume is its purpose.
D is wrong. Falling costs are what make it work.
SC13-10  Answer A. Different elasticities, separate markets.
B is wrong. Identical customers make discrimination pointless.
C is wrong. Cost differences make it ordinary pricing, not discrimination.
D is wrong. Commodities are the hardest case for it.
SC13-11  Answer A. The peak price carries the displaced contribution.
B is wrong. Full cost does not vary by time of day.
C is wrong. Target costing concerns design.
D is wrong. Bundling is a different tactic.
SC13-12  Answer A. Below the seller's own avoidable cost.
B is wrong. The competitor's cost is unknown and not the test.
C is wrong. Pricing below the market is ordinary competition.
D is wrong. Full absorbed cost includes unavoidable costs.
SC13-13  Answer A. Both floors, the market, and the reversal figure.
B is wrong. Full cost alone ignores the market.
C is wrong. The competitor's price alone ignores the company.
D is wrong. The absorption rate is an accounting device.
SC13-14  Answer A. Cost position is largely fixed at design.
B is wrong. A high price invites attack.
C is wrong. Advertising is expensive to sustain.
D is wrong. Newness does not last.
Your turn
@table
Item | Answer
Contribution a unit under skimming | 14.00: 30.00 − 16.00 = 14.00.
First-year operating income under skimming | 560,000: 90,000 × 14.00 = 1,260,000, less fixed costs of 700,000.
Contribution a unit under penetration | 8.00: 24.00 − 16.00 = 8.00.
First-year operating income under penetration | 740,000: 180,000 × 8.00 = 1,440,000, less 700,000.
Which is better in year one | Penetration, by 180,000: 740,000 − 560,000 = 180,000.
Volume at which penetration matches skimming | 157,500 units: skimming's contribution of 1,260,000 divided by penetration's margin of 8.00.
What it tells you | Penetration wins if it can deliver more than 157,500 units, which is 75 per cent more than skimming's 90,000. The whole decision is a judgment about whether the lower price buys that much volume, and nothing in the cost figures can settle it.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P13-01  Answer A. The short-run relevant cost.
B is wrong. Full cost is the long-run floor.
C is wrong. That is the long-run target.
D is wrong. The competitor's price is a market fact, not a floor.
P13-02  Answer A. Full cost and a return.
B is wrong. Variable cost is a short-run floor.
C is wrong. The same objection applies.
D is wrong. Idle capacity is a short-run condition.
P13-03  Answer A. Introduction, growth, maturity, decline.
B is wrong. That is a product's physical life, not its market life.
C is wrong. That is a management cycle.
D is wrong. Those are variables in cost-volume-profit analysis.
P13-04  Answer A. Low volume and no learning yet.
B is wrong. Costs are falling by then.
C is wrong. Costs are lowest at maturity.
D is wrong. Decline raises unit costs, but introduction is the classic answer.
P13-05  Answer A. Inelastic early demand and a genuine lead.
B is wrong. Elastic demand suits penetration.
C is wrong. Falling costs suit penetration.
D is wrong. Established rivals remove the skimming opportunity.
P13-06  Answer A. 30.00 − 16.00 = 14.00.
B is wrong. That is the price.
C is wrong. That is the variable cost.
D is wrong. This adds instead of subtracting.
P13-07  Answer A. 1,260,000 − 700,000 = 560,000.
B is wrong. That is the contribution before fixed costs.
C is wrong. That is the fixed cost.
